description:
`memory.rs:357-393` has two branches that share the same iterator-with-early-return-on-error pattern:

    None => {
        let mut list = Vec::new();
        if let Some(m) = map {
            for val in m.values() {
                match O::deserialize(val) {
                    Ok(obj) => list.push(obj),
                    Err(e) => {
                        return Err(MemoryError(format!(
                            "deserialize {}: {e}", O::TYPE_NAME
                        )))
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        Ok((list, vec![]))
    }
    Some(id_slice) => {
        let mut found = Vec::new();
        let mut not_found = Vec::new();
        for id in id_slice {
            match map.and_then(|m| m.get(id)) {
                Some(val) => match O::deserialize(val) {
                    Ok(obj) => found.push(obj),
                    Err(e) => {
                        return Err(MemoryError(format!(
                            "deserialize {}: {e}", O::TYPE_NAME
                        )))
                    }
                },
                None => not_found.push(id.clone()),
            }
        }
        Ok((found, not_found))
    }

Both branches express 'collect mapped values; on the first deserialize error, bail with a wrapped error'. Idiomatic Rust: `.map(...).collect::<Result<Vec<_>, _>>()?` with `.map_err(|e| MemoryError(format!("deserialize {}: {e}", O::TYPE_NAME)))` inside the map. The None branch becomes:

    None => {
        let list = map
            .map(|m| {
                m.values()
                    .map(|val| {
                        O::deserialize(val).map_err(|e| {
                            MemoryError(format!("deserialize {}: {e}", O::TYPE_NAME))
                        })
                    })
                    .collect::<Result<Vec<_>, _>>()
            })
            .transpose()?
            .unwrap_or_default();
        Ok((list, vec![]))
    }

The Some branch is harder to fold into one `.collect()` because it produces two output vecs (`found` and `not_found`) — a manual loop is reasonable there, but the error case still fits `.map_err(...)?` instead of the explicit `match Err { return Err(...) }` form.

What goes wrong if it stays as-is: nothing functionally. The complaint is readability: the current shape has 3+ levels of nesting (`if let Some(m) { for val in m { match deserialize { Ok ... Err return ... } } }`) and the `Err(e) => return Err(...)` is bytes of boilerplate every implementer of `SetObject` will read. `.collect::<Result<Vec<_>, _>>()` is the canonical 'unzip results' idiom and reads as such.

Lower priority than is_some_and / catchall findings — this is a refactor for clarity, not a behavioural improvement. Filed because the function is a documented teaching example for backend implementors per the module rustdoc ("intended audience: downstream contributors"); the current shape teaches the wrong pattern.
